nicholas sarkozy was not in the paris  court for the verdict into the so-called  magmalian corruption trial but his 13  co-accused were all were found guilty of  illegal campaign financing sarkozy was  sentenced to a year under house arrest  president sarkozy with whom i spoke on  the phone just now asked me to go and  appeal which i will do immediately when  i leave here and of course the judgment  will not be enforceable  nicholas sarkozy failed to be re-elected  as french president in 2012 despite a  lavish campaign the scale of his rallies  was designed to impress but instead it  set off alarm bells  a police investigation accused sarkozy  and his campaign team of spending more  than double the 27 million legal limit  set by the french state and hiring a pr  company called big malian to help cover  up the overspending with a system of  fake invoices  during the trial sarkozy denied any  wrongdoing but the judge in her verdict  said that sarkozy knew how much could be  spent on a presidential campaign because  he'd run before and he knew the law she  also said that he had twice received  written warnings about the risks of  overspending  symbolically it's important to say that  a president  doesn't get any immunity  and has to respond to the law  it is also important because the  campaign  for 2022 is coming so we have to have  this reflection about  how to prevent illegal financing of a  political campaign  sarkozy served one term as president  from 2007 a straight talker with a love  of luxury he was tough on immigration  and crime he retired from politics in  2017. in recent years the political  right wing has struggled in france but  sarkozy's remained prominent for a lot  of french citizens who  vote for the right side nikola sagosi is  seen as this kind of  all-time somehow reassuring figure  the conviction is a further blow to  sarkozy's reputation in march he was  found guilty of corruption in a  different case and he could soon be back  in court he's accused of receiving  illegal campaign funds from the late  libyan leader muhammad gaddafi sarkozy's  legal problems it seems are far from  over natasha butler al jazeera paris
 

